  "narrative": "H121 is the due validation deadline. In TP53-wildtype, KEAP1-wildtype, SMARCA4-wildtype, MSI-stable samples, PTEN loss remains strongly associated with the hypothesized stronger-dependency direction: signed discovery estimate R115 = +0.260 [0.182, 0.338] and independent validation R119 = +0.300 [0.180, 0.420]. Because the estimates are already signed, positive values correspond to more negative raw dependency_composite, i.e. stronger dependency. Both intervals lie wholly above the 0.15 composite-unit threshold, and the validation lower bound remains above threshold even with the stricter independent alpha. Therefore I accept and close H121. This refines the PTEN line: a broad MSI-stable-only or all-context PTEN claim remains uncertain, but when MSI-stability is combined with a clean TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-wildtype genomic background, the PTEN-loss stronger-dependency association is threshold-level and replicated.\n\nThe current supported supplied-dataset findings are: (1) TP53 loss is associated with higher composite scores, i.e. weaker dependency, and is replicated: H13, R1 +0.829 [0.767, 0.891], R26 +0.795 [0.709, 0.881]. (2) KEAP1 loss is associated with weaker dependency and is replicated: H3, R3 +0.810 [0.745, 0.875], R13 +0.738 [0.644, 0.831]. (3) SMARCA4 loss is associated with more negative composite scores, i.e. stronger dependency, and is replicated: H4, R4 +0.804 [0.742, 0.867], R39 +0.827 [0.731, 0.924]. (4) MSI-high is associated with stronger dependency and is exceptionally robust: H111, R105 +0.775 [0.664, 0.885], R106 +0.888 [0.713, 1.064], with threshold-level effects across many lineage, ploidy, serum, matched-data, and co-deletion-exclusion strata. (5) PTEN loss is associated with stronger dependency in restricted clean genomic contexts, notably H67 clean TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-wildtype, validated by R66 +0.263 [0.135, 0.392]; H121 clean plus MSI-stable, now validated by R119; and H134 clean plus MSI-stable plus matched RNA-DNA data, discovery R131 +0.269 [0.179, 0.359]. Narrow accepted PTEN contexts also include H68 MTAP-wildtype and H69 adenocarcinoma-restricted clean contexts, but those are selected from multiple PTEN tests and should not be generalized beyond the dataset or to all PTEN-loss samples.\n\nNegative or unsupported areas include most tested conventional driver/loss variables, the constructed research markers, STK11 loss, MTAP loss, library main effects, and basic omics-completeness indicators. No consistent threshold-level interaction modifiers were supported: effects remained broadly stable against lineage subtype, ploidy, growth rate, Cas9 activity, media serum, EMT, hypoxia, stemness, and common co-deletion contexts, or the interactions were imprecise/rejected. The broad PTEN claims H58 and H133 were not accepted because validation intervals fell below threshold even when point estimates were near or above it.\n\nThe main unresolved line is CDKN2A loss. Across many contexts its signed stronger-dependency point estimates hover around the threshold, e.g. MSI-stable MTAP-wildtype +0.167 [0.083, 0.251], MTAP-wildtype +0.143 [0.059, 0.227], clean MSI-stable MTAP-wildtype +0.134 [0.074, 0.195], and TP53-loss context +0.188 [0.045, 0.332], but no interval is wholly above 0.15. This is not coherent enough to accept CDKN2A as a threshold-level association. It may reflect a weak true effect, partial confounding by MSI or MTAP co-deletion patterns, or noise. Additional arbitrary subgroup cuts are not the right solution. Instead, the two newly registered CDKN2A proposals target the remaining ambiguity more directly: the CDKN2A effect within MSI-high samples to test localization, and a CDKN2A-by-MTAP-loss interaction within MSI-stable samples to test whether MTAP co-deletion modifies the borderline effect. The already active H145/H146 and H147 are also the most informative next tests: they examine whether the accepted clean-context PTEN association survives requiring MTAP wildtype and whether MSI-high's very strong association is independent of common co-deletion patterns jointly. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset; they do not establish causal dependency biology or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 11 synthesis update. H121 accepted and closed: PTEN loss in TP53/KEAP1/SMARCA4-wildtype, MSI-stable context has discovery +0.260 [0.182, 0.338] and validation +0.300 [0.180, 0.420], wholly above 0.15 in stronger-dependency direction. This supports clean-context PTEN but does not rescue broad all-context H58 or broad MSI-stable-only H133. Robust accepted main associations: TP53 loss -> weaker dependency; KEAP1 loss -> weaker dependency; SMARCA4 loss -> stronger dependency; MSI-high -> stronger dependency. PTEN loss -> stronger dependency accepted in clean/restricted contexts (H67, H121, H134, H68, H69), not as a general main effect. CDKN2A remains unresolved/borderline; avoid further arbitrary context cuts. Next high-value tests: active H145/H146 PTEN in clean+MSI-stable+MTAP-wt, H147 MSI-high co-deletion independence, and new CDKN2A proposals: MSI-high-only main effect and MTAP-loss interaction within MSI-stable. No validation requests remain. Interpret all findings as supplied-dataset associations, not causal or generalizable mechanisms."
